ds and phrases. Quantum permutation, Quantum reflection.\nL36@P0-1: 12 TEO BANICA\nL37@P1: Woronowicz’s axioms and theory allow the construction of many interesting examples of\nL38@P1: compact quantum groups. A quite standard procedure, called “liberation and twisting”,\nL39@P1: is that of starting with a compact Lie group G ⊂ UN , and replacing the commutation\nL40@P1: relations ab = ba between the standard coordinates uij : G → C with other relations,\nL41@P1: such as ab = ±ba, abc = cba, abc = ±cba and so on, or with nothing at all.\nL42@P1: The liberation and twisting procedure applies well to ON , UN and other classical Lie\nL43@P1: groups, and allows the construction of liberations O\nL44@P1: +\nL11717@P166: (7) Once again this comes from a result from [14], stating that we have O−1\nL11718@P166: n = G+(Kn),\nL11719@P166: where Kn is the n-dimensional hypercube, having N = 2n vertices.\nL11720@P166: Finally, the stability assertion is clear from the definition of the various products in\u0002volved, from [36], [92]. This is well-known, and we will be back later on to this. \u0003\nL11721@P166: Summarizing, we have a substantial list of examples. We will see in the next section\nL11722@P166: that there are several other interesting examples, coming from the matrix models.\nL12432@P178: Thus by normality of H we have the following formula:\nL12433@P178: (π ⊗ id)∆(xi) = 1 ⊗ xi\nL12434@P178: On the other hand, assuming that we have i ∈ O2, we obtain:\nL12435@P178: (π ⊗ id)∆(xi) = X\nL12436@P178: k\nL12437@P178: X\nL12438@P178: j∈O1\nL12439@P178: vik ⊗ ukj\nL12440@P178: =\nL12441@P178: X\nL12442@P178: k∈O2\nL12443@P178: vik ⊗ xk\nL12444@P178: Multiplying this by vik ⊗1 with k ∈ O2 yields vik ⊗xk = vik ⊗xi, that is to say xk = xi.\nL12445@P178: In other words, xi only depends on the orbit of i. The same is of course true for yi.\nL12446@P178: By using this observation, we can compute the following element:\nL12447@P178: z =\nL12448@P178: X\nL12449@P178: k∈O2\nL1245