neral definition:\nL12495@P180: Definition 12.1. A matrix model for G ⊂ U\nL12496@P180: +\nL12497@P180: N is a morphism of C\nL12498@P180: ∗\nL12499@P180: -algebras\nL12500@P180: π : C(G) → MK(C(T))\nL13480@P190: Now by taking the scalar product with ξ, this gives the first assertion. As for the second\nL13481@P190: assertion, this follows from the first assertion, by summing over ξ = η = ei. \u0003\nL13482@P190: Now back to the Weyl matrix models, let us first compute Tp. We have:\nL13483@P190: Proposition 12.15. We have the formula\nL13484@P190: (Tp)ia,jb =\nL13485@P190: 1\nL13486@P190: N\nL13487@P190: < i1, a1 − a2 > . . . < ip, ap − a1 >< j2, b2 − b1 > . . . < j1, b1 − bp >\nL13488@P190: Z\nL13489@P190: E\nL13490@P190: tr(Wi2−i1,a2−a1UWj1−j2,b1−b2U\nL13491@P190: ∗\nL13492@P190: ). . . tr(Wi1−ip,a1−apUWjp−j1,bp−b1U\nL13493@P190: ∗\nL13494@P190: )dU\nL13495@P190: with all the indices varying in a cyclic way.\nL13912@P195: Theorem 12.21. The construction H → G has the following properties:\nL13913@P195: (1) For a Fourier matrix H = FG we obtain the group G itself, acting on itself.\nL13914@P195: (2) For H 6∈ {FG}, the quantum group G is not classical, nor a group dual.\nL13915@P195: (3) For a tensor product H = H0 ⊗ H00 we obtain a product, G = G0 × G00.\nL13916@P195: Proof. All this material is standard, and elementary, as follows:\nL13917@P195: (1) In the cyclic group case, H = FN , all the objects involved in the construction\nL13918@P195: H → G have an obvious cyclic structure, and we obtain from this G = ZN . We can pass\nL13919@P195: then to the general case by using (3), whose proof is independent of this.\nL13920@P195: (2) This is something more tricky, needing some general study of the representations\nL13921@P195: whose Hopf images are commutative, or cocommutative. For details here, along with a\nL13922@P195: number of supplementary facts on the construction H → G, we refer to [11].\nL13923@P195: (3) This is elementary, the idea being that the tensor products of matrix models are\nL13924@P195: matrix models, and that at the level of corresponding Hopf images, under suitable as\u0002sumptions, the compatibility holds as well. Once again, we refer here to [11]. \u0003\nL13925@P195: Going beyond the above result is an interesting question, and we refer here to [10], and\nL13926@P195: to follow-up papers. There are several computations available here, for the most regarding\nL13927@P195: the deformations of the Fourier models. The unification of all this with the Weyl matrix\nL13928@P195-196: models is a very good question, related to many interesting things.QUANTUM PERMUTATIONS 197\nL13929@P196: References\nL13930@P196: [1] T. Banica, Symmetries of a generic coaction, Math. Ann. 314 (1999), 763–780.\nL13931@P196: [2] T. Banica, Subfactors associated to compact Kac algebras, Integral Equations Operator Theory 39\nL13932@P196: (2001), 1–14.\nL13933@P196: [3] T. Banica, Quantum groups and Fuss-Catalan algebras, Comm. Math. Phys. 226 (2002), 221–232.\nL13934@P196: [4] T. Banica, Quantum automorphism groups of homogeneous graphs, J. Funct. Anal. 224 (2005),\nL13935@P196: 243–280.\nL13936@P196: [5] T. Banica, The planar algebra of a fixed point subfactor, Ann. Math. Blaise Pascal 25 (2018),\nL13937@P196: 247–264.\nL13938@P196: [6] T. Banica, S.T. Belinschi, M. Capitaine and B. Collins, Free Bessel laws, Canad. J. Math. 63 (2011),\nL13939@P196: 3–37.\nL13940@P196: [7] T. Banica and J. Bichon, Free product formulae for quantum permutation groups, J. Inst. Math.\nL13941@P196: Jussieu 6 (2007), 381–414.\nL13942@P196: [8] T. Banica and J. Bichon, Quantum automorphism groups of vertex-transitive graphs of order ≤ 11,\nL13943@P196: J. Algebraic Combin. 26 (2007), 83–105.\nL13944@P196: [9] T. Banica and J. Bichon, Quantum groups acting on 4 points, J. Reine Angew. Math. 626 (2009),\nL13945@P196: 74–114.\nL13946@P196: [10] T. Banica and J. Bichon, Hopf images and inner faithful representations, Glasg. Math. J. 52 (2010),\nL13947@P19